---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="05-lists"
  lessonSlug="02-list-comprehension"
  hook="The map + filter pattern from Think Python §10.7, compressed into one line."
>
  <h2>The shape</h2>
  <pre class="text-sm"><code>[ expression  for  var  in  iterable  if  condition ]</code></pre>

  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    caption="Squares of every even number in 0..n"
    code={`def squares_of_evens(n):
    return [x * x for x in range(n) if x % 2 == 0]

print(squares_of_evens(6))     # [0, 4, 16]
print(squares_of_evens(10))    # [0, 4, 16, 36, 64]`}
  />

  <h2>Pure filter — leave the expression as the variable</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def words_above_length(words, min_len):
    return [w for w in words if len(w) > min_len]

print(words_above_length(["a", "bee", "cat", "donkey"], 2))
# ['bee', 'cat', 'donkey']`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="When NOT to use a comprehension">
    <p>
      Comprehensions are best for <em>build a list from another sequence</em>.
      If you have side effects (printing, file writes), use a regular
      <code>for</code> loop. A comprehension that returns a list of
      <code>None</code>s is a smell.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Equivalent loop version</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`# These two produce the same list.
result_a = [x * x for x in range(10) if x % 2 == 0]

result_b = []
for x in range(10):
    if x % 2 == 0:
        result_b.append(x * x)

print(result_a == result_b)    # True`}
  />
</Lecture>
